Airflow dynamics and aerosol dispersion in both low-speed and turbulent environments are critical current research topics. This paper investigates and evaluates airflow characteristics and aerosol dispersion mechanisms within a fan-powered ventilation system using Large Eddy Simulation (LES). An experimental model of a fan-powered ventilation system was built, and laser diffraction was employed to measure aerosol distribution. Velocity field data from both the model and the simulation show strong agreement. The current study's new contributions include an analysis of airflow and aerosol dispersion driven by two airflow drivers: an infected person's exhalation flow and a fan. We explore how these flows interact and affect each other, how different operating conditions influence airflow and aerosol dispersion, and how aerosol size impacts concentration distribution.
